  "items": [
    {
      "id": "S1",
      "status": "passed",
      "checks": [
        "The quotient is the actual collapse of a nonempty contractible CW subcomplex. The homotopy extension property is obtained from the subcomplex inclusion; both continuous inverse homotopies descend to that quotient. No quotient equivalence is assumed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S2",
      "status": "passed",
      "checks": [
        "ConeAttachment is the actual mapping-cone quotient. The supplied nullhomotopy gives an equivalence to the constant attachment and then an actual wedge with the unreduced suspension. Path cancellation and a path between wedge points are constructed. Compactness is derived from the finite CW assumption; D is not assumed contractible."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S3",
      "status": "passed",
      "checks": [
        "The counterexample is the geometric realization of an arbitrary finite triangulation of the real projective plane, which is the actual antipodal quotient of the real unit 2-sphere. All reduced rational homology groups vanish, whereas its actual integral H_1 gives ZMod 2 in suspension H_2. Two-cone constructions satisfy the original K/v/B premises, and the resulting space is not contractible. This is not a surrogate presentation with assumed invariants."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S4",
      "status": "passed",
      "checks": [
        "Usual barycentric realization, vertex complement, deletion and link have their literal geometric meanings. The radial strong deformation retraction and original inclusion hypothesis establish the link is nonempty. Suspension equivalence and the integral acyclicity criterion use actual singular homology, derived simple connectivity and CW type. Actual oriented integer boundary matrices include the augmentation and both implications of the finite criterion; rational ranks do not replace integral homology."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S5",
      "status": "passed",
      "checks": [
        "The isolated vertex/empty-link case is handled separately by an actual homeomorphism with D disjoint union a point, giving S^0 for contractible D. The nonempty-link case gives its suspension. The concrete two-vertex example meets the weakened premises and shows why the old B hypothesis cannot be reused."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S6",
      "status": "passed",
      "checks": [
        "The witnesses are the explicit finite graphs on Fin 6: a pair of triangles sharing a vertex plus an isolated point, and two disjoint triangles. Actual connected components, a circle homeomorphism and integral homology show the graph realizations are not homotopy equivalent. Explicit deletion, nullhomotopy, simplicial fold, pointed wedge equivalence and basepoint transport give an equivalence of their actual suspensions. The final existential joins both halves for those same graphs."
      ]
    }
  ],
